  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly sunny and dry Fall weather continues.\n\nLatest surface analysis depicted high pressure centered over \nthe Great Lakes and Subtropical Storm Josephine well offshore. \nSkies were mostly clear with light/calm winds across the FA with\nthe exception of the coastline and Eastern Shore where a few \nhigh clouds were noted. Given clear skies and light/calm winds, \ntemps have dropped into the upper 30s to lower 40s for most \ninland locations with low-mid 50s along the coast. Therefore, \nhave lowered temps a bit and now have morning lows in the mid-\nupper 30s across the typically cooler locations of the Piedmont \nand south-central to southern VA. As such, patchy frost is \npossible early this morning across those areas. Along the coast,\nmorning lows around 50F are expected.\n\nJosephine will continue to move NE away from the local area today, \nallowing for clouds to move offshore by around sunrise. This will \nallow for another pleasant Fall day with sunny skies and highs in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. Lows aren't expected to be as cool \ntonight with low-mid 40s for most inland areas and low-mid 50s along \nthe coast largely due to a weak backdoor front which allows winds to \nbecome light (as opposed to calm) and will likely keep most \nlocations from decoupling. High pressure continues to build into the \nregion this weekend which will allow for continued dry and mostly \nsunny weather with highs in the mid-upper 60s to lower 70s each day. \nThe only day that looks to have a noticeable amount of clouds \nin the sky is Fri where partly sunny to occasionally mostly \ncloudy skies are likely across SE VA/NE NC. However, even these \nclouds clear out after sunset. The light/calm winds at night \nwith high pressure overhead will allow for favorable radiational\ncooling at night Fri, Sat, and Sun nights. Each of these nights\nwill have the potential for locally mid 30s inland. However, \nmost areas will likely remain in the upper 30s to lower 40s \ninland. The coolest night still looks to be Fri night/Sat \nmorning where more widespread mid 30s are possible across \nnorthern portions of the area. A few locations may even drop \ninto the lower 30s across the northern tier of counties closer \nto the high. As such, patchy frost will be possible for several \nnights. More widespread frost is also possible, however, \nconfidence is too low to reflect in the forecast at this time. \nWill need to monitor forecast trends in case Frost Advisories \nare need for a portion of the area.\n\nOtherwise, dry weather continues through at least early next week \nwith most model guidance showing no rain through the rest of the \nmonth. If this period of dry weather persists long enough for a dry \ncold front to pass through the region, fire weather concerns could \narise. Fortunately, high pressure is expected to keep winds light \ninto early next week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tidal anomalies through Friday with minor \nto locally moderate tidal flooding forecast.\n\nLow pressure (Josephine) lingering offshore with elevated winds\nand large swells are resulting in tidal anomalies around 2 ft, \nexpected to increase a bit further over the next ~24 hours. \nCoastal Flood Warnings remain in effect for bay-side portions \nof the MD Eastern Shore as well as along the tidal Potomac. \nElsewhere, nuisance to minor tidal flooding is forecast over the\nnext few high tide cycles. Coastal Flood Advisories continue \nthrough today for most areas and have been into Friday, \nprimarily for the western shore of the Chesapeake Bay and \nadjacent tidal rivers.",
